### 🔴 Finding 3 — No Rate Limiting (OWASP A04: Insecure Design / Unrestricted Resource Consumption)

There is no rate limiting anywhere. Every endpoint — including `/users`, which returns PII — can be hit unlimited times per second. This enables credential stuffing, scraping, and denial-of-service.

**Recommended fix:**
```js
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');

const defaultLimiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000,  // 15 minutes
  max: 100,                   // max 100 requests per window per IP
  standardHeaders: true,
  legacyHeaders: false,
  message: { error: 'Too many requests, please try again later.' },
});

const strictLimiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 60 * 1000,
  max: 10,
  message: { error: 'Rate limit exceeded for this resource.' },
});

app.use(defaultLimiter);              // apply globally
app.use('/users', strictLimiter);     // stricter limit on PII endpoints
```

---

### 🔴 Finding 4 — Missing Security Headers (OWASP A05: Security Misconfiguration)

Express, by default, sends an `X-Powered-By: Express` header that fingerprints the server, and omits all defensive headers (CSP, HSTS, X-Frame-Options, etc.).

**Recommended fix:**
```js
const helmet = require('helmet');

app.use(helmet()); // sets ~14 security headers automatically

// Or configure individually for fine-grained control:
app.use(helmet({
  contentSecurityPolicy: {
    directives: {
      defaultSrc: ["'self'"],
      scriptSrc:  ["'self'"],
    },
  },
  hsts: {
    maxAge: 31536000,       // 1 year
    includeSubDomains: true,
    preload: true,
  },
}));

// Explicitly remove the fingerprinting header
app.disable('x-powered-by');
```

